  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/67/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_114.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The Human Torch's training session is interrupted by some friends who say that Captain America is visiting the Glenville antique auto show.&nbsp; Neither Johnny nor Sue can really believe that they barged in on them, just to talk about \"an old comics magazine hero.\"&nbsp; The teenagers rush off to see this for themselves, as none can quite believe that this hero from the pages of comics can be real.\n\nLater, at the auto show, a couple of mobsters attempt to steal one of the antique cars, so the Torch flies after them, only to be brushed aside by...<i>what's this?</i>...Captain America?&nbsp; <b><i>What</i></b><i>?</i>  Where has <i>he</i> been all these years?\n\nAs Cap jumps onto the moving vehicle, Johnny melts the road ahead of them, and the old (<i>and presumably valuable</i>) car sinks into the molten quagmire.&nbsp; Cap is unimpressed and tells the Torch to \"Beat it, you flaming freak!&nbsp; Look how <i>messy</i> you made everything!\"\n\nThe crooks are rounded up by the police, but the two heroes are left bickering with each other... before Cap finally heads over to speak to the adoring crowd, who've come to see \"the greatest thing to come to Glenville in years!\"&nbsp; This makes Johnny feel like chopped liver, as this old hero seems to be stealing all his thunder.\n\nLater, Johnny is steaming about the incident with his girlfriend, Doris Evans.&nbsp; Johnny finds he can't control his anger at the way Doris is drooling over Cap, while still being opposed to his being the Human Torch, and in the process, ruins the Evans' new linoleum flooring.\n\nMeanwhile, Cap is shown breaking the two crooks who tried to steal the antique car <i>out of prison</i>, sending them off in a Ferrari.&nbsp; The police chase after them, and the Torch happens to fly by at that moment, happy for the distraction to take his mind off of Captain America, and in no time, Johnny captures them, where he learns (to his horror) that the whole plan seems to have been cooked up by none other than <i>Captain America himself</i>!&nbsp; The crooks were nothing more than <i>decoys</i>, sent out to provide a distraction so that he could rob a bank!\n\nAnd indeed, at that very moment, Captain America is shown at the Glenville Bank, having easily overpowered the guards.&nbsp; Moments later, he attaches the loot to a lowered cable, and in seconds, he is lifted up to his \"floating sky platform.\"&nbsp; It soon becomes clear from his thoughts that he's not the <i>real</i> Captain America.\n\nBut once up in the air, he becomes easy prey for the flying Human Torch, who uses his flames to disable the platform and force \"Captain America\" to bail out and parachute down.\n\nThe two tangle one final time, with Cap showing remarkable athletic ability, enough to keep him one step ahead of the Torch during their final confrontation, even luring Johnny into an asbestos-lined truck.&nbsp; But the Torch superheats the air inside, causing the truck to burst open and finally corners Cap long enough to pull off the mask!\n\nBut when unmasked, he finds that \"Cap\" is really his old enemy, the Acrobat.&nbsp; As Johnny leaves him for the police, he wonders whatever happened to the <i>real</i> Captain America, who he even sees in his old comics would \"secretly change from army private Steve Rogers to the great C. A.!\"\n\nAnd we're left with the blurb asking for reader feedback.&nbsp; Do they <i>want</i> to see Captain America return?",
  "grade_one_liner": "Not a standout Torch tale, but the Captain America tryout keeps it engaging.\nThe surprise ending doesn't amaze, but it at least gives the issue its spark.",
  "subtitle": "Trying Out a Legend for a New Audience",
  "review_text": "This is one of those stories where entirely staying in [PAGE: scenario] is almost impossible because my \"future memories\" are at least reliable enough to remember that there <i>is</i> a Captain America present in the future (the 21<sup>st</sup> century) -- so the call-to-action at issue's end was, of course, successful.\n\nAnd that tension -- between treating Cap as a bygone figure, as well as presenting him as a possible returning headliner -- quietly shapes the entire issue.\n\nEven though this is blatantly a \"Let's bring back <b>Captain America</b> from the Golden Age of Comics\" exercise, the story still offers a few genuine points of interest to chew on...\n\n[DIVIDER]\nRight off the bat, we're told that there's a surprise ending awaiting us at the end of the book.\n[ASSET: center | SurpriseEnding | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: right | ComicBookCharacter | width=13%] But also right from the beginning, it's clear that Captain America seems to have existed only in the pages of old comic books, and the fact that he's due to make a public appearance at an auto show in Glenville is treated as... <i>well...</i> [SPAN: severity=warning; text=very <i>suspect</i>].\n[BR]\n[BR] In other words... something that has to be <i>seen</i> to be believed.\n\n[ASSET: left | CapArrives | width=20%] The tone is slightly altered one page later at the show, when Johnny simply wonders where Cap has <i>been</i> all these years, as well as wondering why he picked this moment to make a public appearance.\n[BR]\n[BR] But after the attempted heist by the mobsters, the Human Torch sees with his own eyes... [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=Captain America!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThis issue famously shows \"Captain America\" in [SPAN: severity=error; text=red] trunks in every <i>inner</i> depiction, only showing his normal [SPAN: severity=info; text=blue] trunks on the cover (and even <i>that</i> seems to suggest that it was colored red first, and only afterward did someone cover that up with blue, making the final image appear as very dark purple).\n\nI have no explanation for the red shorts, seeing as how the old <i>Captain America Comics</i> from the 1940s all seem to show Cap wearing blue trunks, which casts doubt about what comics Johnny was actually looking at inside the issue (since they all showed the red trunks).\n\nFor a reader encountering this in real time, the mismatch creates a subtle unease, the kind of artistic inconsistency that nudges the reader to question whether this Captain America is truly who he claims to be.\n\nAlso on the cover is the blue star in the middle of the shield.&nbsp; That, too, is incorrect, as it is <i>never</i> depicted that way in the actual comic, where you see only a white star surrounded by a dark color (which might indeed be blue).&nbsp; Even the Golden Age Captain America covers show a white star, though they often show a [SPAN: severity=info; text=blue] stripe alongside the [SPAN: severity=error; text=red] ones (inconsistently drawn on the old issues, as sometimes the blue stripe is on the outside and sometimes in the middle).\n\n[ASSET: right | NoStripes | width=15%] The net takeaway seems to be that, whoever the colorist was for the issue and cover (apparently Stan Goldberg), he was obviously <i>out of practice</i> drawing the character!\n[BR]\n[BR] Even Cap's co-creator, Jack Kirby, wasn't always consistent with his own drawings, as this panel from the upper-right of page 5 shows.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=Where are Cap's stripes?]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n\n[ASSET: left | RobbingABank | width=14%] Midway through the story, it becomes clear that, either something is <i>seriously</i> wrong with Captain America, or that he's turned to crime, as he helps the two mobsters escape from jail to divert the police away from his <i>real</i> objective, that of robbing the Glenville Bank.&nbsp; And at the bottom of page 12, we do indeed learn that this is <i>obviously</i> not the genuine article, as even <i>he</i> seems to regret tarnishing Cap's sterling reputation.\n\n[ASSET: left | NotAfraidOfTheTorch | width=15%] [ASSET: right | MakeAShowOfIt | width=14%] To his credit, \"Cap\" makes a good show of staying one step ahead of the Human Torch, and it's clear to all readers that, <i>whoever</i> he is, he's quite crafty, and <i>very</i> agile.\n[BR]\n[BR] But the Human Torch is no slouch either, and he finally escapes the asbestos-lined truck.\n\nThe \"surprise ending\" turns out to be that \"Captain America\" is actually the Acrobat, who Johnny describes as \"my old enemy.\"&nbsp; I found this a little hard to believe, as his only previous appearance was back in [LINK:1962:ST_106_torch0|ST #106] (a mere eight issues ago), where he persuaded the Human Torch to join with him and form the \"Torrid Twosome\" (and tried to rob a bank <i>that</i> time as well).\n\nIt's a <i>major stretch</i> to frame him as some type of recurring nemesis, but the script leans on that familiarity to make the twist land with more weight than the character really earns, and at least an \"acrobat\" is probably best suited to mimic the motions of the <i>real</i> hero from yesteryear, so there's <i>that</i>...\n\nStill, his motivations remain thin, and the story relies more on the shock value of the reveal than on any established rivalry.\n\nAnd I <i>gotta</i> ask:\n[HANG] * How did he get out of prison so quickly?\n[HANG] * Where did he get the funding for his \"floating sky platform\" we see on pages 12 and 13?\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(But we're obviously not meant to ask such sensible questions like these!)]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nBecause the story was unabashedly a <i>tryout</i> for Captain America, to find out if readers wanted to see his return...\n[ASSET: center | CapTryout | width=67%]\n\n...it <i>intentionally</i> blurs the line a bit, showing Johnny actually reading his old comic books, which showed Captain America was actually Steve Rogers.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=This is a <i>blatant</i> continuity problem] in the sense that the \"real world\" -- the World War II era of the United States in the 1940s -- <b>would not have known</b> that Cap's true identity was Steve Rogers.&nbsp; (Even Johnny states that \"he used to [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=secretly] change...\")\n\nThe script seems willing to bend that detail in order to strengthen the emotional connection between Johnny and the hero he's reading about.\n\nWe simply have to acknowledge the <b>obvious intent</b> of the story, which was to request reader feedback.&nbsp; And as such, there is [SPAN: severity=info; text=no justification] for penalizing the story simply for being what it said it was...\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=The issue <i>openly invites</i> its audience to participate in shaping the future direction of the line, making the story less a self‑contained adventure and more a conversation starter -- an invitation for readers to weigh in on whether this symbol of an earlier era deserves a place in the present one.]\n\nThe plot itself wasn't anything to write home about, but it nevertheless gets extra points for taking a chance to \"test the waters\" (so to speak) and gauge whether or not the readers of the 1960s would be interested in seeing the return of a hero from yesteryear.\n\nMarvel had already brought back the Sub-Mariner, another Golden Age hero, so Lee and Kirby decided to lower the water bucket into the well once again and draw out this old character (who of course was of particular interest to Kirby, who had co-created him all those years earlier).\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=And to its credit], the story also does a good job of reminding the reader about Johnny's new girlfriend, Doris Evans, once again showing how she <i>hates</i> the fact that Johnny Storm is the Human Torch, even calling him a \"menace\" on page 7, after he inadvertently ruins her mother's new linoleum flooring by running across it with flaming feet.\n\nIt was a nice slice-of-life moment that reminds you who the featured character <i>actually</i> is, not to mention a needed anchor, especially in an issue where the guest star threatens to overshadow the book's lead character.\n\n[PAGE: scenario] would say to speculate on whether or not this tryout actually was successful, but in this case, anyone who has any \"future knowledge\" of Marvel Comics already knows the answer -- it follows the familiar pattern of a James Bond-style promise tucked at the end of the adventure...\n\n[SPAN: display=block; severity=success; textalign=center; fontsize=extralarge; fonttype=bold; text=Captain America <i>will</i> return!]",
  "decoder_ring": "I was first exposed to the story in 1977's <i>Captain America</i> #216, where it was reprinted (this time with the red shorts color-corrected to match the blue we're all familiar with).&nbsp; At the time, I had not yet started to read that mag and only bought it because I saw the Human Torch was in it (and was a big fan of the Torch -- same thing happened with <i>Marvel Team-Up</i> #61, where I was first exposed to Spider-Man).&nbsp; Only a later appearance by Iron Man in issue 218 eventually led me to become a regular buyer of <i>Captain America</i>.",
  "highlight": "The Human Torch crosses paths with Captain America -- and the story keeps a quiet surprise waiting at the end."
}